# V-5 — M0-T15 **CERTIFIED** (PASS) · and one escalation that is bigger than T15

## T15: PASS, seven angles

- **Top-level surface** — the guard is the *only* top-level statement (the other two grep hits
  are inside a SQL template literal). Guarding `main()` guards the whole import surface.
- **The observable swap is sound, and stronger than the sibling's.** `main()` runs
  `DATABASE_URL` check → `validateFormulas()` *(logs the marker)* → `checkNoCycles` →
  `evalFileCount` → `new Client(…)`. The marker fires **strictly before any pg.Client exists**,
  so it detects an entered `main()` even where no database is reachable in the code path at all.
  ECONNREFUSED would have been unusable here *and* a weaker detector.
- **My own bidirectional mutation** — `if (true || …)` → test A fails; `if (false && …)` → test
  B fails. Restored from byte-copy, sha256 `632d631c…` identical, `git diff` clean.
- **The two pre-existing importers** — located independently
  (`tests/unit/build/w2_weights_acyclicity.test.ts:34`,
  `scripts/__tests__/catalog_reconciliation.test.ts:2`), re-run post-fix: **15/15 pass.**
- **Mirrored rather than shared `isDirectEntrypoint` — correct, and I would have ruled the same
  way.** D-9 forbade the import and had not been superseded; wiring the seeder's module graph to
  the migrator would mean a future regression in `migrate.ts`'s guard runs the migrator whenever
  the seeder runs — the exact latent coupling this task deletes. It also declined to build on
  uncertified work. Now that V-3 certifies T11, a shared helper is a reasonable *later* refactor;
  it was not the right call at authoring time.

The guard also forecloses the future: once the `bg_cohort` bug is fixed, an import from a
`DATABASE_URL`-live shell *would* have reached `INSERT … ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE` on
`asset_registry`. With this guard it will not. The ordering was lucky; the guard makes luck
irrelevant.

---

## F-SEEDER-DEAD — HIGH. Please read this even though T15 passed.

The builder recorded the `bg_cohort` formula bug as **F-M0T15-A**, framed as *"the unrelated bug
that was masking the write hazard, a one-line fix away from being gone."* True — and the smaller
half.

**The larger half: the canonical seeder for `asset_registry` cannot run at all.** I reproduced it:

```
$ env -i … DATABASE_URL=<unreachable> npx tsx scripts/seed/asset_registry_seed.ts
exit=1
Seed failed: Formula validation failed for bg_cohort: Unknown variables in formula "COHORT_SIZE": COHORT_SIZE
```

It exits 1 on **every** invocation, before touching the database. `bg_cohort` declares
`expected_volume_formula: 'COHORT_SIZE'` (line 526) while supplying the value only as
`expected_volume_inputs` (line 527); `COHORT_SIZE` is absent from `DEFAULTS`.

**Why this is yours and why it is now:** `asset_registry` is the exact control-plane table this
campaign is auditing, and **§8.6 stage 2 (Conform) must write `domain`, `rung`, `target_floor`,
`integrity_check_sql`, partitions and consumers into it for every asset in every rung.**

- If the seeder is the intended writer of that metadata → **every rung's Conform stage is blocked**
  behind a one-line fix nobody has scheduled.
- If migrations are the intended writer instead → the seeder and the live table **diverge
  silently**, which is a GA.1-class registry disagreement.

Either way, **which surface owns registry metadata is a decision that has to be made before R0's
Conform opens, not during it.** Recommend a work item. I did not fix it (I16) and it is outside
T15's scope — the builder was right to report and not touch it; I am escalating the consequence,
not the bug.

— PARĪKṢAKA
